2026-06-05 16:44:22.086 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | Submitting callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x165868140>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x136693620>>, '[protocole 3.docx - LEAD PROJECT]\nIntroduction\nThis protocol establishes a structured framework for the layout and management of the LEAD project\'s community learning gardens. It defines the setup of two distinct and geographically separate demonstration fields, each covering 1 acre, adapted to the specific needs of each population:\nHost Community Field:\xa0Located in Beyogoya village, focused in cash crops (soybeans and sunflowers).\nRefugee Community Field:\xa0Located in the refugee settlement area, dedicated to diversified market gardening crops.\nThis approach integrates a practical pedagogical methodology (70% on-the-job training) allowing for contextualized and adapted learning for each community.\n1. Spatial Architecture of the Demonstration Fields\n1.1 Layout Concept\nThe LEAD project establishes two separate demonstration fields, each of 1 acre (≈ 4000 m²) for each community:\nField No. 1 - Host Community:\xa0Located in the heart of Beyogoya village, accessible on foot by local farmers.\nField No. 2 - Refugee Community:\xa0Established in or near the refugee settlement area (Zone 6) to facilitate daily access.\nEach field is designed as an autonomous learning ecosystem, divided into four functional zones adapted to the specific needs of its community.\n1.2 Spatial distribution and functions\nFIELD NO. 1: HOST COMMUNITY (1 acre - Host Village)\nZone A - Common learning space (10% - 400 m²)\nLearning and production infrastructure\nProtected nursery:\xa0Shaded structure made of local materials for seedling production.\nComposting station:\xa0Multiple pits and maturation areas for demonstrations.\nSecure water point:\xa0Access to a safe water source for watering.\nTraining area:\xa0Shaded area for theoretical sessions and collective analyses.\nInput store:\xa0Secure storage for tools and protective equipment.\nZone B - Soybean & Sunflower Production Plots (75% - 3000 m²)\nCash crops and experimentation\nPlot 1:\xa0Soybean monocrop - Mastery of the standard technical itinerary. \nPlot 2:\xa0Sunflower monocrop - Specific techniques for the crop.\nPlot 3:\xa0Intercropping systems - Soybeans intercropped with maize/sorghum to demonstrate diversification and land optimization.\nPlot 4:\xa0Experimental plots - Technical comparisons (mulching, fertilization, densities) with traditional local practices.\nZone C - Agroforestry and Conservation (15% - 600 m²)\nEcosystem services and sustainability\nMultifunctional living hedges:\xa0Moringa, Leucaena for biomass and protection.\nFruit trees:\xa0Diversification of income and nutritional improvement.\nAnti-erosion structures:\xa0Dikes, "zai pits," grass strips on sloping areas (if applicable).\nFIELD NO. 2: REFUGEE COMMUNITY (1 acre - Settlement Area)\nZone A - Common learning Space (10% - 400 m²)\nLearning infrastructure adapted to vegetable production\nSpecialized nursery:\xa0Shade house for intensive vegetable seedling production seedlings.\nComposting station:\xa0System adapted to vegetable plant waste.\nWater point with basin:\xa0For watering or irrigation and washing vegetables.\nTraining and processing area:\xa0Area for sessions (Here we already have a hangar fitted out by AVSI).\nAdapted storage:\xa0For gardening tools and harvest crates.\nZone B - vegetable production Plots (75% - 3000 m²)\nIntensive market gardening production\nBlock 1 - Solanaceae:\xa0Raised beds for tomatoes, eggplants, peppers.\nBlock 2 - Leafy vegetables:\xa0Continuous production of cabbage, sukuma wiki (collard greens).\nBlock 3 - Legumes/Alliaceae:\xa0Peas and onions in rotation.\nBlock 4 - Various crops:\xa0Okra and experimental plots.\nProductive borders:\xa0Windbreaks and additional production.\nZone C - Agroforestry and Services (15% - 600 m²)\nComplementary production and protection\nFast-growing trees:\xa0Moringa for nutrition and sale of leaves.\nProductive living hedges:\xa0Protection and production of biomass for mulching.\nWater conservation area:\xa0Micro-basins for supplementary irrigation.\n2. Detailed Technical Itineraries\n2.1 Host Community - Cash Crops\nPhase 1: Soil Preparation (Month 1)\nResidue conservation:\xa0Systematic collection for later mulching.\nDeep plowing:\xa020-25 cm for decompaction.\nOrganic amendment:\xa02 - 4,047 t/acre of incorporated compost. \nRidge preparation:\xa0Alignment according to contour lines (if applicable).\nPhase 2: Crop Planting (Month 2)\nSeed selection:\xa0Use of certified seeds or rigorous farmer selection.\nSeed protection:\xa0Preventive treatment (ash or approved products).\nOptimal densities:\nSoybean: 50 cm × 20 cm (2 seeds/hole).\nSunflower: 75 cm × 50 cm (2 seeds/hole).\nPhase 3: Crop management (Month 3)\nWeed management:\xa0Two weedings are recommended (Day 15 and pre-flowering).\nRational fertilization:\xa0If necessary, a moderate application of NPK fertilizer as a top dressing can be demonstrated, in comparison with an application of liquid compost.\nWater conservation:\xa0Apply a layer of mulch after the first weeding to conserve moisture and limit weeds.\nPhase 4: Integrated Pest Management (Month 4)\nWeekly monitoring:\xa0AESA protocol (Agro-Ecosystem Analysis).\nIntervention cascade:\nPreventive methods (rotation, associations).\nBiological control (auxiliaries).\nBotanical pesticides (neem, chili).\nRational chemical intervention (last resort with PPE).\nPhase 5: Harvest and Post-harvest (Month 5)\nMaturity indicators:\xa0Harvest when the soybean leaves turn yellow and fall, and when the back of the sunflower flower turns yellow-brown.\nOptimized post-harvest:\nDrying on raised platforms.\nDemonstrated manual threshing and winnowing.\nStorage in controlled conditions.\nSeed conservation for autonomy.\n2.2 Refugee Community - Vegetable Production\nThe general approach is based on permanent raised beds, enriched with organic matter (compost, manure), which is ideal for all the listed crops.\nOrganization in Rotation Blocks\nDetailed Crop Calendar\nMonths 1-2: Preparation and Nursery establishment', False) with args () and kwargs {} to thread worker queue
