mplementary production and protection\nFast-growing trees:\xa0Moringa for nutrition and sale of leaves.\nProductive living hedges:\xa0Protection and production of biomass for mulching.\nWater conservation area:\xa0Micro-basins for supplementary irrigation.\n2. Detailed Technical Itineraries\n2.1 Host Community - Cash Crops\nPhase 1: Soil Preparation (Month 1)\nResidue conservation:\xa0Systematic collection for later mulching.\nDeep plowing:\xa020-25 cm for decompaction.\nOrganic amendment:\xa02 - 4,047 t/acre of incorporated compost. \nRidge preparation:\xa0Alignment according to contour lines (if applicable).\nPhase 2: Crop Planting (Month 2)\nSeed selection:\xa0Use of certified seeds or rigorous farmer selection.\nSeed protection:\xa0Preventive treatment (ash or approved products).\nOptimal densities:\nSoybean: 50 cm × 20 cm (2 seeds/hole).\nSunflower: 75 cm × 50 cm (2 seeds/hole).\nPhase 3: Crop management (Month 3)\nWeed management:\xa0Two weedings are recommended (Day 15 and pre-flowering).\nRational fertilization:\xa0If necessary, a moderate application of NPK fertilizer as a top dressing can be demonstrated, in comparison with an application of liquid compost.\nWater conservation:\xa0Apply a layer of mulch after the first weeding to conserve moisture and limit weeds.\nPhase 4: Integrated Pest Management (Month 4)\nWeekly monitoring:\xa0AESA protocol (Agro-Ecosystem Analysis).\nIntervention cascade:\nPreventive methods (rotation, associations).\nBiological control (auxiliaries).\nBotanical pesticides (neem, chili).\nRational chemical intervention (last resort with PPE).\nPhase 5: Harvest and Post-harvest (Month 5)\nMaturity indicators:\xa0Harvest when the soybean leaves turn yellow and fall, and when the back of the sunflower flower turns yellow-brown.\nOptimized post-harvest:\nDrying on raised platforms.\nDemonstrated manual threshing and winnowing.\nStorage in controlled conditions.\nSeed conservation for autonomy.\n2.2 Refugee Community - Vegetable Producti