2026-06-05 16:44:33.329 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | Submitting callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x169bc1ac0>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x136693620>>, '[protocole 3.docx - LEAD PROJECT]\nNursery: Tomatoes, eggplants, peppers, cabbages, onions (4-6 weeks)\nDirect sowing:\xa0Okra, peas, sukuma wiki.\nBed preparation:\xa0Raised, enriched with organic matter.\nMonths 2-3: Crop development\nTransplanting:\xa0Transplanting should be done when the nursery plants have 4-6 true leaves. \nOptimized spacing:\nMonths 3-4: Intensive Maintenance\nWater needs:\xa0All these crops require regular watering. Tomatoes, Peppers, Cabbages, and Sukuma Wiki are particularly thirsty for water during fruit or leaf formation. Mulching is the key technique to reduce evaporation.\nFertilization:\nHeavy feeders (Tomatoes, Cabbages, Eggplants, Peppers, Sukuma Wiki): An application of liquid compost ("compost tea") every 2-3 weeks after transplanting will support their strong growth.\nLight feeder (Peas): As a legume, it fixes its own nitrogen and requires little fertilization.\nSpecific maintenance:\nStaking:\xa0Set up stakes for tomatoes and supports for peas early after transplanting. \nPruning:\xa0For tomatoes, remove suckers (new stems at the axils of the leaves) to concentrate energy on fruit production.\n4. Integrated Pest Management (IPM) (Month 4) \nWeekly AESA (Agro-Ecosystem Analysis) is key. Prevention is emphasized.\nCommon pests and IPM solutions:\nAphids\xa0(on Peas, Peppers, Cabbages): Spray with soapy water or a chili/garlic maceration. Encourage ladybugs.\nCaterpillars\xa0(on Cabbages, Tomatoes): Regular manual removal, neem spray.\nDowny mildew\xa0(on Tomatoes): Prevent by good ventilation (pruning, spacing), avoid watering the foliage.\nKey Strategies:\nCrop rotation:\xa0Never plant tomatoes, eggplants, or peppers in the same place for two consecutive seasons. This is the fundamental principle of fertility and soil disease management.\nCompanion planting:\xa0The smell of onions and certain herbs can disrupt harmful insects.\nMonths 3-5: Staggered Harvest\nThe harvesting method is essential for productivity and loss reduction.\nStaggered (continuous) harvest:\nTomatoes, Eggplants, Peppers:\xa0Harvest the fruits as soon as they reach the right color and size to encourage the plant to produce more.\nOkra:\xa0Pick the pods when they are young and tender (5-8 cm long), every 2-3 days.\nSukuma Wiki:\xa0Harvest the outer leaves as needed, leaving the young leaves in the center to continue growing.\nPeas:\xa0Pick the pods when they are well-filled but still tender.\nSingle harvest:\nCabbages:\xa0Harvest when the head is dense and firm.\nOnions:\xa0Harvest when the leaves turn yellow and fall over. Let them dry in the sun for a few days before storing them.\nPost-harvest handling must be gentle, especially for tomatoes and leafy vegetables, to maximize their shelf life and market value.\n3. Integrated Training-Production Calendar\n4. Pedagogical Strategies\n4.1 Methodological Principles\nActive learning:\xa0Each session should begin with a brief explanation (15 min) followed by a long practical session (85 min) where each participant performs the task. \nComparative trials:\xa0Control vs. improved plots.\nEmpowerment:\xa0Each group of participants will be assigned responsibility for one or more beds to encourage ownership and continuous maintenance. \nDocumentation:\xa0Monitoring notebooks for traceability.\n4.2 Animation Tools\nWeekly AESA sessions:\xa0Organize weekly sessions where small groups walk through the field, observe the condition of the crops, insects, soil moisture, and present their findings to the rest of the group for collective decision-making.\nFarmer field schools:\xa0Exchange of experiences between peers.\nCulinary demonstrations:\xa0Linking production-nutrition-health.\n5. Governance and Sustainability\n5.1 Roles and Responsibilities \nTrainers\nFacilitation of technical sessions.\nWeekly monitoring of plots.\nIndividualized support for households.\nDocumentation of practices and results.\nParticipants\nRotational maintenance of common plots.\nApplication of techniques in their own farms.\nActive participation in VSLAs.\nSharing of experiences.\n5.2 Resource Management \nHarvest Distribution\n30%:\xa0Nutritional demonstrations.\n40%:\xa0Collective sale (funding VSLA).\n30%:\xa0Equitable distribution to participants.\nSystem Sustainability\nLocal seed production.\nAutonomy in organic amendments.\nTransmission of knowledge between generations.\nIntegration into local markets.\n6. Monitoring and Evaluation\n6.1 Performance Indicators\nTechnical:\xa0Yields, germination rates, pest incidence.\nEconomic:\xa0Production costs, income generated, VSLA savings.\nSocial:\xa0Participation rates, adoption of practices, community cohesion.\nEnvironmental:\xa0Soil quality, biodiversity, water conservation.\n6.2 Monitoring Tools\nPlot monitoring sheets:\xa0A simple monitoring notebook will be kept for each field, noting the dates of interventions, observations (rain, pests), quantities of products applied, and yields harvested. This tool will serve as a basis for discussions and evaluation of the crop cycle.\nAttendance and participation registers.\nVSLA account books.\nMonthly activity reports.', False) with args () and kwargs {} to thread worker queue
2026-06-05 16:44:33.332 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | #9 got callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x169bc1ac0>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x136693620>>, '[protocole 3.docx - LEAD PROJECT]\nNursery: Tomatoes, eggplants, peppers, cabbages, onions (4-6 weeks)\nDirect sowing:\xa0Okra, peas, sukuma wiki.\nBed preparation:\xa0Raised, enriched with organic matter.\nMonths 2-3: Crop development\nTransplanting:\xa0Transplanting should be done when the nursery plants have 4-6 true leaves. \nOptimized spacing:\nMonths 3-4: Intensive Maintenance\nWater needs:\xa0All these crops require regular watering. Tomatoes, Peppers, Cabbages, and Sukuma Wiki are particularly thirsty for water during fruit or leaf formation. Mulching is the key technique to reduce evaporation.\nFertilization:\nHeavy feeders (Tomatoes, Cabbages, Eggplants, Peppers, Sukuma Wiki): An application of liquid compost ("compost tea") every 2-3 weeks after transplanting will support their strong growth.\nLight feeder (Peas): As a legume, it fixes its own nitrogen and requires little fertilization.\nSpecific maintenance:\nStaking:\xa0Set up stakes for tomatoes and supports for peas early after transplanting. \nPruning:\xa0For tomatoes, remove suckers (new stems at the axils of the leaves) to concentrate energy on fruit production.\n4. Integrated Pest Management (IPM) (Month 4) \nWeekly AESA (Agro-Ecosystem Analysis) is key. Prevention is emphasized.\nCommon pests and IPM solutions:\nAphids\xa0(on Peas, Peppers, Cabbages): Spray with soapy water or a chili/garlic maceration. Encourage ladybugs.\nCaterpillars\xa0(on Cabbages, Tomatoes): Regular manual removal, neem spray.\nDowny mildew\xa0(on Tomatoes): Prevent by good ventilation (pruning, spacing), avoid watering the foliage.\nKey Strategies:\nCrop rotation:\xa0Never plant tomatoes, eggplants, or peppers in the same place for two consecutive seasons. This is the fundamental principle of fertility and soil disease management.\nCompanion planting:\xa0The smell of onions and certain herbs can disrupt harmful insects.\nMonths 3-5: Staggered Harvest\nThe harvesting method is essential for productivity and loss reduction.\nStaggered (continuous) harvest:\nTomatoes, Eggplants, Peppers:\xa0Harvest the fruits as soon as they reach the right color and size to encourage the plant to produce more.\nOkra:\xa0Pick the pods when they are young and tender (5-8 cm long), every 2-3 days.\nSukuma Wiki:\xa0Harvest the outer leaves as needed, leaving the young leaves in the center to continue growing.\nPeas:\xa0Pick the pods when they are well-filled but still tender.\nSingle harvest:\nCabbages:\xa0Harvest when the head is dense and firm.\nOnions:\xa0Harvest when the leaves turn yellow and fall over. Let them dry in the sun for a few days before storing them.\nPost-harvest handling must be gentle, especially for tomatoes and leafy vegetables, to maximize their shelf life and market value.\n3. Integrated Training-Production Calendar\n4. Pedagogical Strategies\n4.1 Methodological Principles\nActive learning:\xa0Each session should begin with a brief explanation (15 min) followed by a long practical session (85 min) where each participant performs the task. \nComparative trials:\xa0Control vs. improved plots.\nEmpowerment:\xa0Each group of participants will be assigned responsibility for one or more beds to encourage ownership and continuous maintenance. \nDocumentation:\xa0Monitoring notebooks for traceability.\n4.2 Animation Tools\nWeekly AESA sessions:\xa0Organize weekly sessions where small groups walk through the field, observe the condition of the crops, insects, soil moisture, and present their findings to the rest of the group for collective decision-making.\nFarmer field schools:\xa0Exchange of experiences between peers.\nCulinary demonstrations:\xa0Linking production-nutrition-health.\n5. Governance and Sustainability\n5.1 Roles and Responsibilities \nTrainers\nFacilitation of technical sessions.\nWeekly monitoring of plots.\nIndividualized support for households.\nDocumentation of practices and results.\nParticipants\nRotational maintenance of common plots.\nApplication of techniques in their own farms.\nActive participation in VSLAs.\nSharing of experiences.\n5.2 Resource Management \nHarvest Distribution\n30%:\xa0Nutritional demonstrations.\n40%:\xa0Collective sale (funding VSLA).\n30%:\xa0Equitable distribution to participants.\nSystem Sustainability\nLocal seed production.\nAutonomy in organic amendments.\nTransmission of knowledge between generations.\nIntegration into local markets.\n6. Monitoring and Evaluation\n6.1 Performance Indicators\nTechnical:\xa0Yields, germination rates, pest incidence.\nEconomic:\xa0Production costs, income generated, VSLA savings.\nSocial:\xa0Participation rates, adoption of practices, community cohesion.\nEnvironmental:\xa0Soil quality, biodiversity, water conservation.\n6.2 Monitoring Tools\nPlot monitoring sheets:\xa0A simple monitoring notebook will be kept for each field, noting the dates of interventions, observations (rain, pests), quantities of products applied, and yields harvested. This tool will serve as a basis for discussions and evaluation of the crop cycle.\nAttendance and participation registers.\nVSLA account books.\nMonthly activity reports.', False) with args () and kwargs {} from queue
