2026-06-05 16:47:59.913 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | Submitting callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x31f9a02c0>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x136693620>>, '[SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE TRAINING GUIDE.docx - INTRODUCTION TO SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE]\nBy the end of the session, you should be able to\nUse an alternative farming system which is   economically viable, environmentally friendly and socially just\n  Recognize soil as a living resource that deserves care and feeding\n1.2 Sustainable agriculture practices\nSustainable agriculture practices may include reducing or eliminating the use of agricultural chemicals. Examples of farming systems that fall within the scope of sustainable agriculture are Low-input agriculture, organic farming, and perm culture.\n1.3 Organic agriculture\nOrganic agriculture looks at a farm as a living organism in which all the components (the soil, minerals, organic matter, micro-organisms, insects and other animals, plants and humans) interact to form one whole.\n1.4 Meaning of sustainable agriculture\nThis is a farming system that uses naturally and locally available materials to satisfy continuous human needs for food, income, energy and shelter while protecting the environment and conserving the natural resource base at the same time. Sustainable Agriculture describes farming systems that maintain agricultural productivity through reduced environmental degradation and are economically viable in both the short and long term for improved livelihoods.\nThe detailed components of the farm vary according to local growing conditions, but are likely to include:\nLeguminous crops, Root crops, Vegetables, Fruits, Cereals, Oil seeds\nCash crops, Livestock, Small enterprise livestock: rabbits, bees, fish\n1.5 Principles of Sustainable Agriculture\nSustainable agriculture is based on four main principles:\nDiversity- having many different plants and animals within the farm. Diversity is seen at four levels of crops, animals, trees and livelihood activities. While planning for your farm there is need to mix the different elements that will bring out diversity on the farm. Diversity can further be detailed by looking at the different crop varieties and animal breeds. Diversity on the farm contributes to other elements of agroecology like synergies.\nConnectivity/Synergy- Everything is connected to everything else i.e. every element of the farm must be placed at the place in relation to other elements to enable recycling of energy resources. In this regard “There is no waste to waste” in agroecology. i. e what seems to be waste from one element is an input to a different element. For example, the cow dung that is waste from animals is manure (input) for plants. One of the principles of agroecology farm planning and design is connecting the output of one element with the input of another. This is because every element has outputs and inputs.\nLiving soil – Fertile, productive soils with many organisms (micro and macro). Soil is a living thing that needs to be looked after well. It requires feeding like any other living thing. Soil can be fed through composting, green manuring, plant tea, manure tea. Compost is a complete soil meal that contains nitrogen (legumes and animal waste), potassium (wood ash), magnesium, phosphorus (wood ash), calcium, trace minerals and carbon (dry matter). On the other hand, green manuring involves use of fresh green grass, dry grass or animal waste.\nRecycling – Emphasis on recycling of available resources such as energy, nutrients, water, etc.\nKeeping animals according to their nature e.g. providing adequate housing and feeding. Livestock offer both agro ecological and wider environmental benefits. As part of balanced, well-managed agroecosystems, they can be highly beneficial to long-term sustainability.\nEnvironmental pollution and depletion of natural resources is discouraged.\nHealthy society should be maintained, thus prices, wages and living conditions of producers should be fair and just.\nIntegrity - organic food must be produced by organic methods and be protected from contamination and mingling with non-organic products, there must be a system in place and actions taken to ensure that consumers of organic products get what they pay for.\n1.6 Other guiding principles\nWork where it pays –Farmers should practice it for a purpose and invest their time in enterprises which they know will benefit them.\nEncourage cooperation and not competition – all enterprises on the farm should support each other.\nAvoid doing work that nature can do for you –Example carrying out water harvesting and mulching the garden to reduce on frequent weeding, planting of trees on the farm for building poles, fodder, firewood and others.\nGive every item on your farm to perform at least two functions –Example old leaking jerry can be turned into a feeding trough for poultry or piggery, trees on the farm can be used for building poles, firewood, shade and fodder.\nUse resources intensively but sustainably – Example is putting a small piece of land to vegetative production with high maintenance of soil fertility, one is able to obtain a variety of highly valued vegetables.\n1.7 Objectives of sustainable agriculture\nTo produce food of high nutritional quality in sufficient quantity\nTo maintain and increase long term fertility of soils\nTo use, as much as possible, renewable resources in locally organized agricultural systems.\nTo use, as much as possible, with a closed system with regard to organic matter and nutrient elements\nTo work, as much as possible, with materials and substances which can be re-used or recycled, either on the farm or elsewhere\nTo maintain the genetic diversity and biodiversity of the agricultural system and its surroundings including the protection of habitats of indigenous and wild plants and animals\n1.8 Common practices that illustrate SA principles\n1.8.1 Diversity\nAgro-forestry\nMultiple cropping\nOn farm seed production\nCrop rotation\nIntegration of crops and animal', False) with args () and kwargs {} to thread worker queue
