2026-06-05 16:48:12.483 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | Submitting callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x31f9287c0>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x136693620>>, '[SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE TRAINING GUIDE.docx - INTRODUCTION TO SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE]\n3.3. Soil components\nSoil is made up of air, water, minerals, plants, animals. It 3.4 is living when it can support proper plant growth and quality yields\n3.4 Soil degradation\nSoil is part of a farmer’s capital and must care for it protectively just as a businessman care for his investment.  If soils are not properly looked after they become degraded and are not able to support much plant and animal life. Some factors that lead to soil degradation include: lack of recycling of organic matter, population pressure, over-cultivation, deforestation, pollution, ignorance, poor farming methods, overgrazing, overstocking, indiscriminate bush burning, and soil erosion.\n3.5 Soil and water conservation\nSoil and water conservation are those appropriate measures and practices applied on a given land unit to maintain and preserve soil fertility, optimum water harvesting and retention to ensure sustainable production of crops and livestock. Soil and water conservation are a technique of controlling soil erosion and good use of water by all users sustainably. The effective soil and water management practices can improve soil fertility and increase yields in a sustainable way. These techniques conserve soil and water, preserve soil moisture and/or drain water sustainably to avoid soil erosion and depletion of soil nutrient. Different soil and water conservation measures are used in agroecological farming including terracing, Continuous Contour Trenches (Fanya juu and Fanya chini, bench terraces, stone terraces), zai pits, grass/contour bounds, use of cover crops, mulching, water harvesting measures (underground tanks, water ditches,) etc. Well applied practices for water and soil conservation benefit farmers by;\nReduction of the amounts and velocity of surface runoff\nRemoval of unwanted excessive runoff easily\nMaintenance and improvement of soil fertility\nConservation and retention of soil moisture\nPrevention or minimizing the effects of raindrop impacts on the soil.\n3.6 Soil erosion\nThis is the washing away of the top fertile soil due to running water and wind. It is the most common cause of soil fertility loss and hence soil degradation in East Africa. It is one of the major problems that if not controlled results in the loss of the topsoil and decline in the land productivity. Over time, a number of practices and technologies to control erosion have been developed. These involve the creation of barriers along contours which slow the speed of water runoff down the slope and therefore encourage percolation of water into the soil.\n3.7 Factors influencing soil erosion\nAmount and intensity of rainfall\nSlope of the land\nType of the soil\nLand use\n3.8 Principles of erosion control\nDe-energize rain drops (with mulch, trees and cover crops)\nSlow down water flow across the land (contours, L-bridges, ditches, bands, trashes, etc.)\nIncrease soil porosity (creating aeration)\nPrevent small channels from increasing to form gullies (line pegs / twigs, sacks of sand)\nHarvest all the water that drops on the farm (tanks, ditches, dams, etc.)\n3.9 Practices used in soil and water conservation\nThe major categories of soil and water conservation measures are;\nCultural practices (Agronomic)\nPhysical practices\n3.9.1 Cultural practices (Agronomic)\nThe cultural practices (measures) are done as part of the normal farming practices by ensuring optimum plant cover over soil to protect it from rain drops and maintaining good soil structure from effective water infiltration. Cultural practices include;\nMulching (covering soil with crop trash)\nCorrect spacing to ensure optimum plant population\nTimely/early planting (so that ground is well covered by the time heavy rain starts, planting along contours\nApplication of organic manures (to improve on soil structure and texture)\nPlanting crops on ridges\nMinimum tillage, Fallowing (resting land under bush/vegetation\nOptimum stocking rates, \nCover crops (especially legumes), \nGrass strips and trash lines, \nStrip cropping\nIntercropping\nCrop rotations\nWind breaks\nAgro forestry\n3.9.2 Physical practices\nTerraces\nStone lines\nSoil bunds (Fanya juu and Fanya Chini)\nWater retention ditches\nCut off drains\nCheck dams\nThe Terraces, stone lines, and soil bunds require a farmer to first locate contour lines using a simple locally made instrument called an “A-Frame”.\n3.10 A - Frame', False) with args () and kwargs {} to thread worker queue
