2026-06-05 16:48:21.572 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | Submitting callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x169ba8980>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x136693620>>, '[SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE TRAINING GUIDE.docx - INTRODUCTION TO SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE]\nStep 3: Repeat the same exercise to locate the next position where another contour will be marked using the peg above as the reference. This is done until you get to the lower boundary of your land.\nStep 4: Using the A-frame, locate and mark contours starting at the reference points (see how to use an A-frame to establish contours earlier).\nThe interval or distance from one contour to another will depend on the steepness of the slope. On steep slopes the distance between contours is short. On gently sloping land, the distance is longer. If the contours are too close together, it means the slope is very steep and the piece of land should not be used for production of annual crops as soil erosion will be a very serious problem. It is recommended that such land be used for establishing woodlots and tree crops such as avocados, apples or mangoes were minimum tillage will be practiced.\n3.11 Fanya Chini and Fanya Juu trenches\nThese are established along contours and are very good for soil and water conservation purposes. A farmer should therefore establish them and also maintain them regularly by removing soil, which will have been carried by running water into the dugout canals.\nThey are established as follows:\n3.11.1 Fanya Chini (literally ‘Make down’ in Kiswahili)\nThis is constructed at the upper boundary of the field to enable a farmer to harvest surface running water and soil carried from the upper areas and also to protect his fields from that runoff.\nEstablish a contour line with the use of the A-Frame.\nusing a hoe; dig a canal along the contour line. The canal should be about 60 cm wide and 60cm deep depending on the amount of run-off.\nScoop out the soil from the canal and place it on the lower side of the canal.\n3.11.2 Fanya Juu (`make ‘up’)\nThese are constructed along contours in the middle of fields to stop soil erosion within the fields.  A Fanya juu is also constructed at the lowermost part of the field to stop loss of water and soil to the neighboring fields.\nStep 1\tEstablish a contour line by the use of an A-Frame\nStep 2\tUse a hoe or spade to dig a canal along the contour line as in step 2 above\nStep 3 Scoop out the soil from the canal and place it on the upper side to form a band\nStep 4 Plant crops like Napier (elephant) grass along the top of the band to keep the soil in place.\n3.12 Water harvesting structures and management methods\nWater harvesting is the collection of running water either from roofs, ground surfaces or seasonal swamps for domestic use, crops and livestock.\nFarmers should always remember that water is an essential resource on the farm and increases agricultural production greatly.\n3.12.1 How water can be harvested?\nRain water can be harvested by using roofs, rocks, big tree trunks and trenches and then stored in Tanks (plastic, metallic, cement, and polythene), pots, dams, ponds, water basins around tree crops and planting pits.\nSome illustrations', False) with args () and kwargs {} to thread worker queue
