2026-06-05 16:54:01.367 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | Submitting callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x31f9a02c0>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x136693620>>, '[REVISED SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE TRAINING GUIDE_2026.pdf - TABLE OF CONTENTS]\n• \n1 part well-matured compost ......................................................................................... 65 \n• \n1 part sand (to improve drainage) ................................................................................. 65 \n6.7 Sowing and seedling management ....................................................................................... 65 \n6.7.1 Sowing .................................................................................................................................. 65 \n1. \nWater the seedbed well before sowing. ............................................................................. 65 \n2. \nMake shallow furrows (about 1 cm) spaced 10 to 15 cm apart. Sowing in rows makes \nweeding and thinning easier. ...................................................................................................... 65 \n3. \nFor very fine seeds (like cabbage), mix them with dry sand to ensure a more uniform \ndistribution. .................................................................................................................................. 65 \n4. \nSow the seeds sparsely in the furrows. .............................................................................. 65 \n5. \nGently cover the seeds with a thin layer of sieved substrate. Do not bury them too \ndeep. .............................................................................................................................................. 65 \n6. \nLightly firm the surface and water gently with a fine-rosed watering can. .................... 65 \n7. \nCover the seedbed with a thin layer of mulch (dry grass) to maintain moisture and \nprotect the seeds from the force of the water. Remove this mulch as soon as the first \nseedlings appear. .......................................................................................................................... 65 \n6.7.2 Nursery Management .......................................................................................................... 65 \n• \nWatering: Water the seedlings morning and evening, using a fine watering can to avoid \ndamaging them. The soil should remain moist but not waterlogged. ..................................... 65 \n• \nThinning: When the seedlings have developed 2 to 3 true leaves, pull out the weaker \nones to give more space to the more vigorous ones. ................................................................ 66 \n• \nWeeding: Gently remove weeds by hand as soon as they appear. .................................. 66 \n• \nHardening off: This is a crucial step to prepare the seedlings for the shock of \ntransplanting. About one to two weeks before the planned transplanting date: ................... 66 \n1. \nGradually reduce the shade by removing part of the cover each day. ............................. 66 \n2. \nGradually reduce the frequency of watering, without letting the plants wilt completely.', False) with args () and kwargs {} to thread worker queue
2026-06-05 16:54:01.368 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | #9 got callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x31f9a02c0>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x136693620>>, '[REVISED SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE TRAINING GUIDE_2026.pdf - TABLE OF CONTENTS]\n• \n1 part well-matured compost ......................................................................................... 65 \n• \n1 part sand (to improve drainage) ................................................................................. 65 \n6.7 Sowing and seedling management ....................................................................................... 65 \n6.7.1 Sowing .................................................................................................................................. 65 \n1. \nWater the seedbed well before sowing. ............................................................................. 65 \n2. \nMake shallow furrows (about 1 cm) spaced 10 to 15 cm apart. Sowing in rows makes \nweeding and thinning easier. ...................................................................................................... 65 \n3. \nFor very fine seeds (like cabbage), mix them with dry sand to ensure a more uniform \ndistribution. .................................................................................................................................. 65 \n4. \nSow the seeds sparsely in the furrows. .............................................................................. 65 \n5. \nGently cover the seeds with a thin layer of sieved substrate. Do not bury them too \ndeep. .............................................................................................................................................. 65 \n6. \nLightly firm the surface and water gently with a fine-rosed watering can. .................... 65 \n7. \nCover the seedbed with a thin layer of mulch (dry grass) to maintain moisture and \nprotect the seeds from the force of the water. Remove this mulch as soon as the first \nseedlings appear. .......................................................................................................................... 65 \n6.7.2 Nursery Management .......................................................................................................... 65 \n• \nWatering: Water the seedlings morning and evening, using a fine watering can to avoid \ndamaging them. The soil should remain moist but not waterlogged. ..................................... 65 \n• \nThinning: When the seedlings have developed 2 to 3 true leaves, pull out the weaker \nones to give more space to the more vigorous ones. ................................................................ 66 \n• \nWeeding: Gently remove weeds by hand as soon as they appear. .................................. 66 \n• \nHardening off: This is a crucial step to prepare the seedlings for the shock of \ntransplanting. About one to two weeks before the planned transplanting date: ................... 66 \n1. \nGradually reduce the shade by removing part of the cover each day. ............................. 66 \n2. \nGradually reduce the frequency of watering, without letting the plants wilt completely.', False) with args () and kwargs {} from queue
