2026-06-05 16:54:07.388 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | Submitting callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x169c2c100>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x136693620>>, '[Protocol for the layout and management of demonstration plots_2026.pdf - Introduction]\nProtocol for the layout and management of demonstration plots \nLEAD Project \nIntroduction \nThis protocol establishes a structured framework for the layout and management of the LEAD \nproject\'s community learning gardens. It defines the setup of two distinct and geographically \nseparate demonstration fields, each covering 1 acre, adapted to the specific needs of each \npopulation: \n• \nHost Community Field: focused in cash crops . \n• \nRefugee Community Field: Located in the refugee settlement area, dedicated to \ndiversified market gardening crops. \nThis approach integrates a practical pedagogical methodology (70% on-the-job training) \nallowing for contextualized and adapted learning for each community. \n1. Spatial Architecture of the Demonstration Fields \n1.1 Layout Concept \nThe LEAD project establishes two separate demonstration fields, each of 1 acre (≈ 4000 m²) \nfor each community: \n• \nField No. 1 - Host Community: Located in their  village, accessible on foot by local \nfarmers. \n• \nField No. 2 - Refugee Community: Established in or near the refugee settlement area \nto facilitate daily access. \nEach field is designed as an autonomous learning ecosystem, divided into four functional \nzones adapted to the specific needs of its community. \n1.2 Spatial distribution and functions \nFIELD NO. 1: HOST COMMUNITY (1 acre - Host Village) \nZone A - Common learning space (10% - 400 m²) \nLearning and production infrastructure \n• \nProtected nursery: Shaded structure made of local materials for seedling production. \n• \nComposting station: Multiple pits and maturation areas for demonstrations. \n• \nSecure water point: Access to a safe water source for watering. \n• \nTraining area: Shaded area for theoretical sessions and collective analyses. \n• \nInput store: Secure storage for tools and protective equipment. \nZone B – cash crops Production Plots (75% - 3000 m²) \nCash crops and experimentation \n• \nPlot 1: Crop A monocrop - Mastery of the standard technical itinerary.  \n• \nPlot 2: Crop B monocrop - Specific techniques for the crop. \n• \nPlot 3: Intercropping systems – Crop A intercropped with maize/sorghum to \ndemonstrate diversification and land optimization. \n• \nPlot 4: Experimental plots - Technical comparisons (mulching, fertilization, densities) \nwith traditional local practices. \nZone C - Agroforestry and Conservation (15% - 600 m²) \nEcosystem services and sustainability \n• \nMultifunctional living hedges: Moringa, Leucaena for biomass and protection. \n• \nFruit trees: Diversification of income and nutritional improvement. \n• \nAnti-erosion structures: Dikes, "zai pits," grass strips on sloping areas (if \napplicable). \nFIELD NO. 2: REFUGEE COMMUNITY (1 acre - Settlement Area) \nZone A - Common learning Space (10% - 400 m²) \nLearning infrastructure adapted to vegetable production \n• \nSpecialized nursery: Shade house for intensive vegetable seedling production \nseedlings. \n• \nComposting station: System adapted to vegetable plant waste. \n• \nWater point with basin: For watering or irrigation and washing vegetables. \n• \nTraining and processing area: Area for sessions (). \n• \nAdapted storage: For gardening tools and harvest crates. \nZone B - vegetable production Plots (75% - 3000 m²) \nIntensive market gardening production \n• \nBlock 1 - Solanaceae: Raised beds for tomatoes, eggplants, peppers. \n• \nBlock 2 - Leafy vegetables: Continuous production of cabbage, sukuma wiki (collard \ngreens). \n• \nBlock 3 - Legumes/Alliaceae: Peas and onions in rotation. \n• \nBlock 4 - Various crops: Okra and experimental plots. \n• \nProductive borders: Windbreaks and additional production. \nZone C - Agroforestry and Services (15% - 600 m²) \nComplementary production and protection \n• \nFast-growing trees: Moringa for nutrition and sale of leaves. \n• \nProductive living hedges: Protection and production of biomass for mulching. \n• \nWater conservation area: Micro-basins for supplementary irrigation. \n2. Detailed Technical Itineraries \n2.1 Host Community - Cash Crops \nPhase 1: Soil Preparation (Month 1) \n• \nResidue conservation: Systematic collection for later mulching. \n• \nDeep plowing: 20-25 cm for decompaction. \n• \nOrganic amendment: 5-10 t/ha of incorporated compost. \n• \nRidge preparation: Alignment according to contour lines (if applicable). \nPhase 2: Crop Planting (Month 2) \n• \nSeed selection: Use of certified seeds or rigorous farmer selection. \n• \nSeed protection: Preventive treatment (ash or approved products). \n• \nOptimal densities: \no Soybean: 50 cm × 20 cm (2 seeds/hole). \no Sunflower: 75 cm × 30 cm (2 seeds/hole). \nPhase 3: Crop Management (Month 3) \n• \nWeed management: Two weedings are recommended (Day 15 and pre-flowering). \n• \nRational fertilization: If necessary, a moderate application of NPK fertilizer as a top \ndressing can be demonstrated, in comparison with an application of liquid compost. \n• \nWater conservation: Apply a layer of mulch after the first weeding to conserve \nmoisture and limit weeds. \nPhase 4: Integrated Pest Management (Month 4) \n• \nWeekly monitoring: AESA protocol (Agro-Ecosystem Analysis). \n• \nIntervention cascade: \n1. Preventive methods (rotation, associations). \n2. Biological control (auxiliaries). \n3. Botanical pesticides (neem, chili). \n4. Rational chemical intervention (last resort with PPE). \nPhase 5: Harvest and Post-harvest (Month 5) \n• \nMaturity indicators: Harvest when the soybean leaves turn yellow and fall, and \nwhen the back of the sunflower flower turns yellow-brown. \n• \nOptimized post-harvest: \no Drying on raised platforms. \no Demonstrated manual threshing and winnowing. \no Storage in controlled conditions. \no Seed conservation for autonomy. \n2.2 Refugee Community - Vegetable Production \nThe general approach is based on permanent raised beds, enriched with organic matter \n(compost, manure), which is ideal for all the listed crops. \nOrganization in Rotation Blocks \nBlock \nFamily \nCrops \nCharacteristics \nBlock 1 Solanaceae \nTomatoes, Eggplants, Peppers High nutrient demand \nBlock 2 Brassicaceae & Leafy Cabbages, Sukuma Wiki \nNitrogen requirement \nBlock 3 Legumes & Alliums Peas, Onions \nFixers/Improvers \nBlock 4 Various crops \nOkra, Trials \nExperimentation zone \nDetailed Crop Calendar \nMonths 1-2: Preparation and Nursery establishment', False) with args () and kwargs {} to thread worker queue
