2026-06-05 16:54:07.802 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | Submitting callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x167e46900>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x136693620>>, '[Protocol for the layout and management of demonstration plots_2026.pdf - Introduction]\n• \nNursery: Tomatoes, eggplants, peppers, cabbages, onions (4-6 weeks) \n• \nDirect sowing: Okra, peas, sukuma wiki. \n• \nBed preparation: Raised, enriched with organic matter. \nMonths 2-3: Crop development \n• \nTransplanting: Transplanting should be done when the nursery plants have 4-6 true \nleaves, preferably at the end of the day to reduce water stress.  \n• \nOptimized spacing: \nCrop \nRecommended Spacing \nImportant Notes \nTomatoes \n60 cm x 50 cm \nStaking is essential. Planting onions or \nbasil nearby can help repel certain pests.  \nEggplants & \nPeppers \n60 cm x 60 cm \nThey appreciate heat. A thick mulch is \nvery beneficial for conserving soil \nmoisture. \nCabbages \n60 cm x 50 cm \nRequires rich soil. Crop rotation is \ncrucial to avoid soil-borne diseases. \nOnions \n20 cm x 10 cm \nTransplant the young plants. A light \nearthing up can be beneficial. \nOkra \n75 cm x 40 cm \nSow 2-3 seeds per hole, then keep only \nthe most vigorous plant (thinning). \nPeas \nDouble rows spaced 60 cm apart, \nwith 10 cm between the peas. \nRequires support (netting, stakes, twigs). \nFixes nitrogen, excellent for the soil. \nSukuma Wiki 40 cm x 30 cm \nVery productive crop. Responds well to \nregular applications of liquid compost. \nMonths 3-4: Intensive Maintenance \n• \nWater needs: All these crops require regular watering. Tomatoes, Peppers, Cabbages, \nand Sukuma Wiki are particularly thirsty for water during fruit or leaf formation. \nMulching is the key technique to reduce evaporation. \n• \nFertilization: \no Heavy feeders (Tomatoes, Cabbages, Eggplants, Peppers, Sukuma Wiki): An \napplication of liquid compost ("compost tea") every 2-3 weeks after \ntransplanting will support their strong growth. \no Light feeder (Peas): As a legume, it fixes its own nitrogen and requires little \nfertilization. \n• \nSpecific maintenance: \no Staking: Set up stakes for tomatoes and supports for peas early after \ntransplanting.  \no Pruning: For tomatoes, remove suckers (new stems at the axils of the leaves) \nto concentrate energy on fruit production. \n4. Integrated Pest Management (IPM) (Month 4)  \nWeekly AESA (Agro-Ecosystem Analysis) is key. Prevention is emphasized. \n• \nCommon pests and IPM solutions: \no Aphids (on Peas, Peppers, Cabbages): Spray with soapy water or a chili/garlic \nmaceration. Encourage ladybugs. \no Caterpillars (on Cabbages, Tomatoes): Regular manual removal, neem spray. \no Downy mildew (on Tomatoes): Prevent by good ventilation (pruning, \nspacing), avoid watering the foliage. \n• \nKey Strategies: \no Crop rotation: Never plant tomatoes, eggplants, or peppers in the same place \nfor two consecutive seasons. This is the fundamental principle of fertility and \nsoil disease management. \no Companion planting: The smell of onions and certain herbs can disrupt \nharmful insects. \nMonths 3-5: Staggered Harvest \nThe harvesting method is essential for productivity and loss reduction. \n• \nStaggered (continuous) harvest: \no Tomatoes, Eggplants, Peppers: Harvest the fruits as soon as they reach the \nright color and size to encourage the plant to produce more. \no Okra: Pick the pods when they are young and tender (5-8 cm long), every 2-3 \ndays. \no Sukuma Wiki: Harvest the outer leaves as needed, leaving the young leaves \nin the center to continue growing. \no Peas: Pick the pods when they are well-filled but still tender. \n• \nSingle harvest: \no Cabbages: Harvest when the head is dense and firm. \no Onions: Harvest when the leaves turn yellow and fall over. Let them dry in \nthe sun for a few days before storing them. \nPost-harvest handling must be gentle, especially for tomatoes and leafy vegetables, to \nmaximize their shelf life and market value. \n3. Integrated Training-Production Calendar \nMonth Training Module \nPractical Activities \nSkills Developed \n1 \nOrientation & Planning \nLayout, composting, soil \nanalysis \nDesign of productive \nspaces \n2 \nSeeds & Multiplication \nNursery, sowing, \ntransplanting \nPropagation techniques \n3 \nFertility Management \nWeeding, mulching, \nfertilization \nMaintaining soil health \n4 \nIntegrated Pest \nManagement (IPM) \nAESA, botanical \npesticides \nDiagnosis and intervention \n5 \nHarvest & Post-harvest \nConservation techniques \nLoss reduction \n6 \nMarketing & Sustainability Economic analysis, VSLA Agricultural business \nmanagement \n4. Pedagogical Strategies \n4.1 Methodological Principles \n• \nActive learning: Each session should begin with a brief explanation (15 min) \nfollowed by a long practical session (85 min) where each participant performs the \ntask.  \n• \nComparative trials: Control vs. improved plots. \n• \nEmpowerment: Each group of participants will be assigned responsibility for one or \nmore beds to encourage ownership and continuous maintenance.  \n• \nDocumentation: Monitoring notebooks for traceability. \n4.2 Animation Tools \n• \nWeekly AESA sessions: Organize weekly sessions where small groups walk through \nthe field, observe the condition of the crops, insects, soil moisture, and present their \nfindings to the rest of the group for collective decision-making. \n• \nFarmer field schools and exchange visits: Exchange of experiences between peers. \n• \nCulinary demonstrations: Linking production-nutrition-health. \n5. Governance and Sustainability \n5.1 Roles and Responsibilities  \nTrainers \n• \nFacilitation of technical sessions. \n• \nWeekly monitoring of plots. \n• \nIndividualized support for households. \n• \nDocumentation of practices and results. \nParticipants \n• \nRotational maintenance of common plots. \n• \nApplication of techniques in their own farms. \n• \nActive participation in VSLAs. \n• \nSharing of experiences. \n5.2 Resource Management  \nHarvest Distribution \n• \n30%: Nutritional demonstrations. \n• \n40%: Collective sale (funding VSLA). \n• \n30%: Equitable distribution to participants. \nSystem Sustainability \n• \nLocal seed production (Agribusiness dealer). \n• \nAutonomy in organic amendments. \n• \nTransmission of knowledge between generations. \n• \nIntegration into local markets. \n6. Monitoring and Evaluation \n6.1 Performance Indicators \n• \nTechnical: Yields (The quantity produced for each crop must be recorded and \ntracked), germination rates (The germination test must be carried out before sowing or \nplacing the seeds in the nursery), pest incidence (must be done everytime trough \nAESA Walk). \n• \nEconomic: Production costs, income generated, VSLA savings. \n• \nSocial: Participation rates, adoption of practices, community cohesion. \n• \nEnvironmental: Soil quality, biodiversity, water conservation. \n6.2 Monitoring Tools \n• \nPlot monitoring sheets: A simple monitoring notebook will be kept for each field, \nnoting the dates of interventions, observations (rain, pests), quantities of products \napplied, and yields harvested. This tool will serve as a basis for discussions and \nevaluation of the crop cycle. \n• \nAttendance and participation sheets. \n• \nVSLA account books. \n• \nWeekly and Monthly activity reports.', False) with args () and kwargs {} to thread worker queue
2026-06-05 16:54:07.804 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | #1 got callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x167e46900>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x136693620>>, '[Protocol for the layout and management of demonstration plots_2026.pdf - Introduction]\n• \nNursery: Tomatoes, eggplants, peppers, cabbages, onions (4-6 weeks) \n• \nDirect sowing: Okra, peas, sukuma wiki. \n• \nBed preparation: Raised, enriched with organic matter. \nMonths 2-3: Crop development \n• \nTransplanting: Transplanting should be done when the nursery plants have 4-6 true \nleaves, preferably at the end of the day to reduce water stress.  \n• \nOptimized spacing: \nCrop \nRecommended Spacing \nImportant Notes \nTomatoes \n60 cm x 50 cm \nStaking is essential. Planting onions or \nbasil nearby can help repel certain pests.  \nEggplants & \nPeppers \n60 cm x 60 cm \nThey appreciate heat. A thick mulch is \nvery beneficial for conserving soil \nmoisture. \nCabbages \n60 cm x 50 cm \nRequires rich soil. Crop rotation is \ncrucial to avoid soil-borne diseases. \nOnions \n20 cm x 10 cm \nTransplant the young plants. A light \nearthing up can be beneficial. \nOkra \n75 cm x 40 cm \nSow 2-3 seeds per hole, then keep only \nthe most vigorous plant (thinning). \nPeas \nDouble rows spaced 60 cm apart, \nwith 10 cm between the peas. \nRequires support (netting, stakes, twigs). \nFixes nitrogen, excellent for the soil. \nSukuma Wiki 40 cm x 30 cm \nVery productive crop. Responds well to \nregular applications of liquid compost. \nMonths 3-4: Intensive Maintenance \n• \nWater needs: All these crops require regular watering. Tomatoes, Peppers, Cabbages, \nand Sukuma Wiki are particularly thirsty for water during fruit or leaf formation. \nMulching is the key technique to reduce evaporation. \n• \nFertilization: \no Heavy feeders (Tomatoes, Cabbages, Eggplants, Peppers, Sukuma Wiki): An \napplication of liquid compost ("compost tea") every 2-3 weeks after \ntransplanting will support their strong growth. \no Light feeder (Peas): As a legume, it fixes its own nitrogen and requires little \nfertilization. \n• \nSpecific maintenance: \no Staking: Set up stakes for tomatoes and supports for peas early after \ntransplanting.  \no Pruning: For tomatoes, remove suckers (new stems at the axils of the leaves) \nto concentrate energy on fruit production. \n4. Integrated Pest Management (IPM) (Month 4)  \nWeekly AESA (Agro-Ecosystem Analysis) is key. Prevention is emphasized. \n• \nCommon pests and IPM solutions: \no Aphids (on Peas, Peppers, Cabbages): Spray with soapy water or a chili/garlic \nmaceration. Encourage ladybugs. \no Caterpillars (on Cabbages, Tomatoes): Regular manual removal, neem spray. \no Downy mildew (on Tomatoes): Prevent by good ventilation (pruning, \nspacing), avoid watering the foliage. \n• \nKey Strategies: \no Crop rotation: Never plant tomatoes, eggplants, or peppers in the same place \nfor two consecutive seasons. This is the fundamental principle of fertility and \nsoil disease management. \no Companion planting: The smell of onions and certain herbs can disrupt \nharmful insects. \nMonths 3-5: Staggered Harvest \nThe harvesting method is essential for productivity and loss reduction. \n• \nStaggered (continuous) harvest: \no Tomatoes, Eggplants, Peppers: Harvest the fruits as soon as they reach the \nright color and size to encourage the plant to produce more. \no Okra: Pick the pods when they are young and tender (5-8 cm long), every 2-3 \ndays. \no Sukuma Wiki: Harvest the outer leaves as needed, leaving the young leaves \nin the center to continue growing. \no Peas: Pick the pods when they are well-filled but still tender. \n• \nSingle harvest: \no Cabbages: Harvest when the head is dense and firm. \no Onions: Harvest when the leaves turn yellow and fall over. Let them dry in \nthe sun for a few days before storing them. \nPost-harvest handling must be gentle, especially for tomatoes and leafy vegetables, to \nmaximize their shelf life and market value. \n3. Integrated Training-Production Calendar \nMonth Training Module \nPractical Activities \nSkills Developed \n1 \nOrientation & Planning \nLayout, composting, soil \nanalysis \nDesign of productive \nspaces \n2 \nSeeds & Multiplication \nNursery, sowing, \ntransplanting \nPropagation techniques \n3 \nFertility Management \nWeeding, mulching, \nfertilization \nMaintaining soil health \n4 \nIntegrated Pest \nManagement (IPM) \nAESA, botanical \npesticides \nDiagnosis and intervention \n5 \nHarvest & Post-harvest \nConservation techniques \nLoss reduction \n6 \nMarketing & Sustainability Economic analysis, VSLA Agricultural business \nmanagement \n4. Pedagogical Strategies \n4.1 Methodological Principles \n• \nActive learning: Each session should begin with a brief explanation (15 min) \nfollowed by a long practical session (85 min) where each participant performs the \ntask.  \n• \nComparative trials: Control vs. improved plots. \n• \nEmpowerment: Each group of participants will be assigned responsibility for one or \nmore beds to encourage ownership and continuous maintenance.  \n• \nDocumentation: Monitoring notebooks for traceability. \n4.2 Animation Tools \n• \nWeekly AESA sessions: Organize weekly sessions where small groups walk through \nthe field, observe the condition of the crops, insects, soil moisture, and present their \nfindings to the rest of the group for collective decision-making. \n• \nFarmer field schools and exchange visits: Exchange of experiences between peers. \n• \nCulinary demonstrations: Linking production-nutrition-health. \n5. Governance and Sustainability \n5.1 Roles and Responsibilities  \nTrainers \n• \nFacilitation of technical sessions. \n• \nWeekly monitoring of plots. \n• \nIndividualized support for households. \n• \nDocumentation of practices and results. \nParticipants \n• \nRotational maintenance of common plots. \n• \nApplication of techniques in their own farms. \n• \nActive participation in VSLAs. \n• \nSharing of experiences. \n5.2 Resource Management  \nHarvest Distribution \n• \n30%: Nutritional demonstrations. \n• \n40%: Collective sale (funding VSLA). \n• \n30%: Equitable distribution to participants. \nSystem Sustainability \n• \nLocal seed production (Agribusiness dealer). \n• \nAutonomy in organic amendments. \n• \nTransmission of knowledge between generations. \n• \nIntegration into local markets. \n6. Monitoring and Evaluation \n6.1 Performance Indicators \n• \nTechnical: Yields (The quantity produced for each crop must be recorded and \ntracked), germination rates (The germination test must be carried out before sowing or \nplacing the seeds in the nursery), pest incidence (must be done everytime trough \nAESA Walk). \n• \nEconomic: Production costs, income generated, VSLA savings. \n• \nSocial: Participation rates, adoption of practices, community cohesion. \n• \nEnvironmental: Soil quality, biodiversity, water conservation. \n6.2 Monitoring Tools \n• \nPlot monitoring sheets: A simple monitoring notebook will be kept for each field, \nnoting the dates of interventions, observations (rain, pests), quantities of products \napplied, and yields harvested. This tool will serve as a basis for discussions and \nevaluation of the crop cycle. \n• \nAttendance and participation sheets. \n• \nVSLA account books. \n• \nWeekly and Monthly activity reports.', False) with args () and kwargs {} from queue
