2026-06-05 16:54:19.545 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | Submitting callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x169c2c100>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x136693620>>, "[REVISED SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE TRAINING GUIDE.docx - SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE]\nPREFACE\nThere is a massive demand today for agricultural products grown without excessive reliance on chemicals. A large portion of the world's population is interested in a holistic approach to health and is willing to invest in products from sustainable agricultural practices.\nHistorically, Uganda, like many nations, has practiced traditional agriculture in harmony with nature. However, to meet the needs of a growing population, programs encouraged the use of chemical fertilizers to increase yields, which sometimes had negative impacts on soil and consumer health. Today, to reverse this trend, many countries, including Uganda, are turning to more sustainable agricultural practices, which has led to improved organic exports and farmer incomes. The Ugandan government actively supports this transition through programs aimed at improving agricultural production, soil health, and the adoption of sustainable scientific techniques.\nThe objective of this training manual is to provide a detailed overview of the principles and practices of Climate-Smart Agriculture (CSA), specifically adapted to the context of the LEAD project in Palabek. The ambition of this initiative is to make farmers, researchers, extension workers, and all other stakeholders aware of the knowledge and practices based on the principles of self-sufficiency and environmental protection.\nWith this conviction, I congratulate the dedicated team that contributed to this compilation. I hope this manual will be an effective training tool for participants, trainers, and all project partners. I am confident that, through this publication, we can take another step towards an autonomous and sustainable agriculture.", False) with args () and kwargs {} to thread worker queue
