2026-06-05 16:54:23.275 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | Submitting callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x169c1b900>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x136693620>>, "[REVISED SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE TRAINING GUIDE.docx - SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE]\nIntroduction to Climate-Smart Agriculture for Resilient Farming\n0.1 Chapter Objectives\nBy the end of this session, participants will be able to:\nUnderstand the objectives of the LEAD project and their role in its success.\nDefine Climate-Smart Agriculture (CSA) and explain why it is suited to the challenges of Palabek.\nIdentify the fundamental principles of CSA and how they apply in the community learning gardens.\n0.2 The LEAD Project: Our Shared Vision\nThe LEAD (Local, Empowerment and Development) project is implemented in the Palabek refugee settlement and host communities of Lamwo district to combat chronic food insecurity and poverty. Our overall goal is to empower you, refugees and host community members, by providing practical skills in agriculture and finance for a more secure and sustainable future.\nTogether, we aim to:\nImprove food and nutritional security: By equipping you with the skills to produce your own food, ensuring sufficient and nutritious meals for your families.\nStrengthen resilience to climate shocks: By enhancing the community's ability to withstand environmental challenges such as droughts and unpredictable rains through the adoption of Climate-Smart Agriculture.\nPromote economic empowerment: By enabling you to transition from subsistence farming to market-oriented production, through financial training and Village Savings and Loan Associations (VSLA).\nStrengthen community cohesion: By fostering peaceful coexistence and collaboration between refugees and host communities through joint agricultural and business projects.\nTo achieve these goals, the project relies on two community learning gardens, which are our outdoor classrooms. One is located in Beyogoya village for the host community and focuses on cash crops, while the other, in the settlement area, is dedicated to diversified market gardening for the refugee community.", False) with args () and kwargs {} to thread worker queue
2026-06-05 16:54:23.276 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | #10 got callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x169c1b900>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x136693620>>, "[REVISED SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE TRAINING GUIDE.docx - SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE]\nIntroduction to Climate-Smart Agriculture for Resilient Farming\n0.1 Chapter Objectives\nBy the end of this session, participants will be able to:\nUnderstand the objectives of the LEAD project and their role in its success.\nDefine Climate-Smart Agriculture (CSA) and explain why it is suited to the challenges of Palabek.\nIdentify the fundamental principles of CSA and how they apply in the community learning gardens.\n0.2 The LEAD Project: Our Shared Vision\nThe LEAD (Local, Empowerment and Development) project is implemented in the Palabek refugee settlement and host communities of Lamwo district to combat chronic food insecurity and poverty. Our overall goal is to empower you, refugees and host community members, by providing practical skills in agriculture and finance for a more secure and sustainable future.\nTogether, we aim to:\nImprove food and nutritional security: By equipping you with the skills to produce your own food, ensuring sufficient and nutritious meals for your families.\nStrengthen resilience to climate shocks: By enhancing the community's ability to withstand environmental challenges such as droughts and unpredictable rains through the adoption of Climate-Smart Agriculture.\nPromote economic empowerment: By enabling you to transition from subsistence farming to market-oriented production, through financial training and Village Savings and Loan Associations (VSLA).\nStrengthen community cohesion: By fostering peaceful coexistence and collaboration between refugees and host communities through joint agricultural and business projects.\nTo achieve these goals, the project relies on two community learning gardens, which are our outdoor classrooms. One is located in Beyogoya village for the host community and focuses on cash crops, while the other, in the settlement area, is dedicated to diversified market gardening for the refugee community.", False) with args () and kwargs {} from queue
