2026-06-05 16:55:25.376 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | Submitting callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x169ba8c80>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x136693620>>, '[SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE TRAINING GUIDE.docx - INTRODUCTION TO SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE]\nCHAPTER TWO\n                                                            FARM PLANNING\n2.1 Objective\nThe trainees will be able to design the farm systematically and use available resources effectively and efficiently to realize expected outcome\n2.2. Meaning of farm planning\nThis is the arranging / growing various crops and / or keeping animals on different plots of land according to the nature of land. This means putting land into different uses so that it is utilized fully without damaging the physical properties of the soil. It aims at maximum utilization of land for higher returns.\n2.3 Enterprise selection\nTo select the enterprise:\nThe farmer should have some knowledge about it.\nThe farmer should have somewhere to consult in case of problems.\nThe enterprise should have more than one benefit to the farmer and community around\nThe enterprise is long lived.\nThe enterprise can be self-sustaining (Sustainable)\n2.4 Farm structures and buildings\nFarm buildings include: residential houses, farm offices, cattle shed, milking parlors, piggery units, poultry houses, implement shed, stores, and granaries.\nBuildings should be located on high ground, in the center of the farm, near public roads and should not be very far from source of water.\n2.5 Farm integration and nutrient recycling\nFarm integration is the proper selection and arrangement of different enterprises on the farm in such a way that these enterprises support each other and in so doing enable the farmer to achieve his goals at a low-cost input, while ensuring continuous productivity of the system. Example of farm integration and nutrient recycling\n2.6 Benefits of farm integration\nAchievement of nutrient recycling of all organic matter in the field which is very important in the practice of organic farming.  \nControlled introduction of external inputs into the farm which makes the farm more sustainable\nOrganic wastes are put to best use like generation of energy through bio gas which is used for cooking and lighting in a home. \nNothing or very little goes to waste as there is optimum recycling of nutrients e.g. vegetable remains are given to rabbits, which produce urine and dung which go back to the garden to make the soil more fertile and produce better vegetables.\nSoil fertility is enhanced which contributes to better life of the plants, livestock and the small insects (animals) within the soil by use of animal dung and chicken droppings\nNutrients that would have been lost in deep layers of the soil by the water that passes through it (leaching)is trapped by roots of trees and brought back to top soil through dropping leaves where there are further utilized by crops (This is only possible if the integration has a component of trees or agro forestry)\nA farmer will depend less or not all on external inputs like fertilizers and pesticides\nA farmer will be able to recycle crop residue, animal manure and urine, kitchen waste within the farm.\nA farmer will try as much as possible to harvest water from runoff use and other sources available in the farm.\nA farmer will have firewood, poles for building, shade trees produced in the farm.\n2.7 Ways of cycling nutrients in the farm\nUse of cover crops like legumes which are nitrogen fixing plants like lablab, beans, peas, etc\nPlanting deep-rooted plants (agro forestry) which pump nutrients from the underground to the top for use by the plants\nUsing external inputs of organic origin like animal (cow, goat, and sheep) dung poultry droppings\nUsing renewable energy sources e.g. water, solar lighting, bio-gas, fuel wood and windmills.', False) with args () and kwargs {} to thread worker queue
